<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Lu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lushan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Lu (Lushan) is a mountain range in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, near Jiujiang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-poyang">Poyang Lake</a>. In the late fourth century the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huiyuan">Huiyuan</a> founded the Donglin Temple there, making it a major centre of Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and poets and painters celebrated its scenery for centuries. In the twentieth century its resort of Guling hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and Communist Party meetings, and it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-lu.jpg" alt="Mount Lu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16137-Lushan_(31834519847).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
